  "body": "**This changes no result.** No number produced by any run already made moves because of this patch: on the four fits we measured, 11.33 million written nodes, the two rules that need no extra input find nothing to invalidate, and the third needs the dataset to state the volume shape, which the scroll specification those runs used does not. It is a correctness fix to what a written mesh is allowed to say, and the cost of applying it is measured below rather than asserted: at most 0.02 lamina spacings on a median of 11.86, on 0.18 per cent of the nodes.\r\n\r\n`save_mesh` transforms the spiral grid into scroll coordinates and invalidates a node when its z leaves `[z_begin, z_end)`. That is the only check. A node whose y or x leaves the volume keeps the raw output of the transform and is written where the surface goes, and a node that comes back non finite survives too, because every comparison against NaN is false. `save_tifxyz` then counts both as vertices, since validity there is `np.any(zyxs != -1, axis=-1)`.\r\n\r\nTwo things follow, and the second one is the one that made us look.\r\n\r\n**A mesh can state surface where the scroll has no voxels.** Those nodes enter `bbox` and `area_vx2`, so the metadata says the segment reaches somewhere the volume does not go.\r\n\r\n**One non finite node makes `meta.json` unreadable.** All six `bbox` components become NaN and `json.dump` writes the bare literal `NaN`. RFC 8259 has no such token: Python's `json.loads` accepts it because it extends the standard, `serde_json` and Go's `encoding/json` refuse the document, and `nlohmann::json`, which volume-cartographer parses `meta.json` with, has no NaN in its grammar either. The failure is not one bad vertex, it is a file that does not open. We have not put such a file in front of VC3D: this is a statement about the parsers, not about a session.\r\n\r\n## What we measured\r\n\r\nFour fits of PHerc0826 over z 9000 to 9300, three on GPU and one on CPU, 11.33 million written nodes in total. The scroll is 16920 by 8169 by 8169 voxels in z, y, x.\r\n\r\n| run | valid nodes | with y or x outside the volume | non finite |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| A | 1,493,368 | 0 | 0 |\r\n| A2 | 1,371,343 | 0 | 0 |\r\n| A3 | 1,521,992 | **8,129** (0.53%) | 0 |\r\n| R1 | 1,467,865 | 18 (0.0012%) | 0 |\r\n\r\nWinding 207 of run A3 writes 1,572 of its 16,731 valid nodes with y out to 8367, against a width of 8169. The set is not the same from one run to the next, and no node in any of the four has a negative coordinate.\r\n\r\nThe non finite path is **latent**: zero nodes in 11.33 million. It is reproducible on demand, not observed in the wild. Injecting one NaN into a real winding is enough to produce the `NaN` literal in `meta.json`, and that is what the first test does.\r\n\r\n## What it costs, which is the part a maintainer needs\r\n\r\nThis is a correctness fix, and we would rather you did not have to guess whether it also moves results. It does not.\r\n\r\nWe use these meshes to measure run to run agreement: the median distance between the surfaces two fits of the same input write, in lamina spacings of 16.0 voxels. Dropping the out of volume nodes moves it like this:\r\n\r\n| pair | nodes compared | dropped | before | after |\r\n|---|---|---|---|---|\r\n| A vs A2 | 1,470,016 | 0 | 3.3740 | 3.3740 |\r\n| A vs A3 | 1,540,186 | 2,826 | 11.8586 | **11.8372** |\r\n| A2 vs A3 | 1,457,523 | 49 | 12.8128 | 12.8123 |\r\n\r\nAt most **0.02 spacings**, on nodes that are 0.18 per cent of the worst pair, against a measure that is a median.\r\n\r\nAnd on the runs themselves the patch changes nothing at all: the two rules that need no extra input find zero non finite and zero negative nodes there, and the third needs the dataset to state the volume, which the scroll spec those runs used does not. No number of any run already made moves because of this patch.\r\n\r\n## The patch\r\n\r\nThree commits, one claim each.\r\n\r\n1. `tifxyz`: a node with a non finite component is stored as the `(-1, -1, -1)` sentinel that every reader in this tree already understands, so the tifs, the vertex count, `area_vx2` and `bbox` all state the same thing. `merge_concat_runs.py` already does exactly this to its grids on the line before it calls `save_tifxyz`; one caller defending itself against the writer is the reason to put it in the writer.\r\n2. `spiral-fitting`: `save_mesh` invalidates a node that is not finite, a node with a negative y or x, and a node outside the scroll's bounding box. The bound is `base_shape_zyx` from `spiral-scroll.json`, which `fit_session` already describes as the shape of the volume coordinate domain of Spiral surfaces, which a checkpoint is already refused for disagreeing with, and which `save_combined_preview` already stamps into the preview's `meta.json`. A dataset that does not state it keeps today's behaviour on that bound alone. The writer cannot invent the volume, and we would rather it did not guess.\r\n3. `test`: three cases and a 5,636 byte fixture.\r\n\r\nNothing else is touched, nothing is renamed, nothing nearby is tidied.\r\n\r\n## The test and its fixture\r\n\r\nThe fixture is eleven rows by ten columns cut verbatim out of winding 207 of one of those fitted meshes: 90 valid nodes, 36 of them with y between 8169.1 and 8200.4, so the defect is visible in the fixture's own numbers. The coordinates are the float32 the fit wrote, printed so they read back bit for bit. It is a crop of **our** mesh, not of published data: it cannot be re-derived without running a fit, and we say so rather than implying it is reproducible from the open bucket.\r\n\r\nThe second and third cases replay those nodes through the real `save_mesh`, as if the transform had returned them, with `save_tifxyz` captured instead of writing. The second asserts that what is written is exactly the nodes inside the scroll, unchanged, and nothing else; it then runs the same export with no `base_shape_zyx` and asserts the outside nodes are still written, which states what the bound depends on and stops the first assertion from passing on a crop that happened to have nothing outside it.\r\n\r\n```\r\nPYTHONPATH=spiral-fitting spiral-fitting/.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q \\\r\n  spiral-fitting/tests/test_mesh_node_validity.py\r\n```\r\n\r\nBefore the two patches, with the test in place:\r\n\r\n```\r\nFAILED test_a_node_that_is_not_finite_is_not_a_valid_vertex\r\n       meta.json carries the literal NaN, which RFC 8259 does not allow\r\nFAILED test_nodes_outside_the_scroll_are_not_written_as_surface\r\n       TypeError: save_mesh() got an unexpected keyword argument 'base_shape_zyx'\r\nFAILED test_a_node_that_is_not_finite_is_never_written_as_surface\r\n       128 of 11340 written nodes are not finite\r\n3 failed in 1.22s\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter:\r\n\r\n```\r\n3 passed in 1.35s\r\n```\r\n\r\n`tests/test_spiral_helpers.py` is unaffected.\r\n\r\n## What this does not do\r\n\r\n- `save_combined_preview` carries the same z only expression for the interactive preview surface that VC3D and Lasagna read. We left it alone because we measured the mesh and not the preview. The same helper covers it in one line if you want it in this pull request.\r\n- It does not touch the splice: a spliced vertex comes from patch geometry, which is a different question from what the transform put on the grid.\r\n- It does not say what a renderer currently does with an out of volume node. We measured what the file states, not what each reader makes of it.\r\n- It does not change the fit. Nothing here runs during optimisation.\r\n\r\n## Three things are yours to decide\r\n\r\n1. Is `base_shape_zyx` the bound you want, or would you rather the fit take the volume shape from the scroll zarr when `paths.scroll_zarr` is set? We chose the scroll spec because it is present for every fit and the zarr is optional, but the spec key is the one your own README does not document.\r\n2. Do you want the same two lines in `save_combined_preview`?\r\n3. Should the scroll specification section of `spiral-fitting/README.md` gain a line for `base_shape_zyx`? It is parsed, validated, checkpoint checked and now load bearing, and the README does not mention it.",
